## What we sell (the one-liner)

[One or two sentences: what you sell, to whom, and the outcome it drives.
This anchors how pains get mapped to use cases.]

<!-- e.g. "[Company] is a [category] platform for [audience]. It helps
[buyer] [do the core job] without [the usual pain]." -->

---

## Use cases (Step 3 picks EXACTLY 3 from this list)

The generator may only build cards from use cases listed here — it must not
invent new ones. Give each a stable name plus the pain shape it answers, so
the "Maps to" line can be written honestly.

1. **[Use case name]** — [what it does] — [answers the pain of: ...]
2. **[Use case name]** — [what it does] — [answers the pain of: ...]
3. **[Use case name]** — [what it does] — [answers the pain of: ...]
4. **[Use case name]** — [what it does] — [answers the pain of: ...]
<!-- Add as many as you genuinely have. The skill narrows to 3 per doc. -->

---

## Optional framing band (Step 4)

If your product has a natural stage / maturity / lifecycle framing worth
showing as a visual band, describe it here. If not, write "N/A" and the
skill skips the band entirely.

<!-- e.g. "Lifecycle: Assess → Pilot → Rollout → Scale. Most prospects
enter at Assess." Or just: "N/A" -->

- Band label: <!-- FILL IN or N/A -->
- Stages: <!-- FILL IN -->
- When to include it: <!-- e.g. "Include for technical buyers evaluating
  a phased rollout; skip for single-team quick wins." -->

