## Competitors

Who you come up against, and the honest reason you win or lose. Used to read
competitive pressure in the transcript — never to trash a competitor in the
doc itself.

- **[Competitor 1]** — [how we win / where we lose]
- **[Competitor 2]** — [how we win / where we lose]

---

## Pricing — what's safe to say?

Default posture is **no pricing on the 1-pager** (see Guardrail 3). Only
override here, explicitly, if something is safe to put in writing.

<!-- e.g. "Never put numbers on the doc. It's fine to say 'usage-based,
scales with volume.'" Default: leave pricing off entirely. -->

---

## Proof points we're allowed to use

Only claims listed here may appear in the doc (Guardrail 4). If you don't
have permission to name a customer, use a shape-of-deal reference instead.

- **Named, cleared for external use:** <!-- e.g. "Acme Corp (logo + quote
  approved)" or "none yet" -->
- **Shape-of-deal (anonymized) references OK:** <!-- e.g. "a customer with a
  $50M project", "a 200-person eng org" -->
- **Metrics we can cite:** <!-- e.g. "cut cycle time from 3 weeks to 3 days"
  — only if defensible -->

---

## Things we must never claim

Hard "do not write" list (Guardrail 7). The generator checks this before
writing anything that sounds like a commitment.

- <!-- e.g. No roadmap promises ("coming next quarter"). -->
- <!-- e.g. No SLA / uptime commitments. -->
- <!-- e.g. No security/compliance claims beyond [what's certified]. -->
- <!-- e.g. No pricing commitments beyond the pricing section above. -->

